# Navigation instruction control for the InternVLA-N1 DualVLN primitive.
#
#   operation = "set"  -> queue `instruction` for the robot to execute
#   operation = "get"  -> read the current instruction and client state
#   operation = "stop" -> retire the active plan and hold zero
#
# Handing the request to the robot is queued: the handler records it and the
# ROS side drains it, because the blocking model step happens on the inference
# thread.  "set" then *waits* for that episode to end and answers with its
# terminal status ("finished", or "stopped" for an operator stop); only a
# rejected/gated/unbound request, or one that hits `set_block_timeout_s`,
# returns while the task is still in force.  This is deliberate: a plan node
# must live as long as the task, or Pilot sees a completed plan in the first
# millisecond and dispatches the same instruction again.  "get" never blocks.
# There is no task identifier: the server is stateless apart from the frame
# history, which the next request clears with `reset`.
#
# "set" also reports "already-finished" as status "finished" when the same
# instruction is re-issued within `finish_reissue_grace_s` of a completion: the
# echo must not restart the walk.
#
# status values reported by "get":
#   "awaiting-instruction"  no task is in force; the model is not asked
#   "idle"/"running"/...    the client's own state while a task is in force
#   "blocked"               the local depth proximity gate is closed: execution
#                           is halted, running=false, no model requests are made.
#                           detail carries reason/nearest/stop distance.
#   "held"                  (from "set") the gate is closed, so the instruction
#                           was remembered but not started.  It starts by itself
#                           once the gate reopens; no second "set" is needed.
string operation
string instruction
---
bool accepted
bool running
string instruction
string status
string detail
